Research and analysis. 
Research questions and further Analysis (Related to research questions, methods, testing e.t.c)
This section documents the research direction, evaluation criteria, testing procedures, and comparative analysis of the implemented minimal computing system.
The research combines simulation-based evaluation with comparative benchmarking.
The implemented computer will first be tested and measured independently, then compared with selected open-source minimal computing system.
The purpose is to investigate how architectural differences affect the computational cost of executing equivalent algorithms with identical input data.
The research focuses on simulated architectural behaviour rather than physical processor speed or power consumption.
Research questions are as follows: 
1. How does the developed system perform when executing representative computational workloads?
2. How does its performance compare with selected minimal computing systems under equivalent workloads?
3. Which architectural characteristics explain the observed performance differences?
Metrics that are used to compare systems: 
This research adopts: This research adopts clock-cycle count, executed instruction count, instructions per cycle, and cycles per instruction from that evaluation approach.
All metrics: 
	• Correctness — whether the benchmark produces the expected result.
	•	Execution cycles / clock ticks — total cycles required to finish the workload.
	•	Dynamic instruction count — total instructions actually executed, including repeated loop instructions.
	•	CPI (Cycles Per Instruction) = execution cycles / dynamic instructions.
	•	IPC (Instructions Per Cycle) = dynamic instructions / execution cycles.
	•	Dynamic branch count — number of branch/jump instructions executed. Hutchens also records branches separately from branch-prediction misses.
	•	Memory reads — number of data-memory read operations.
	•	Memory writes — number of data-memory write operations.
	•	Total memory accesses = reads + writes.
	•	Static instruction count / machine-code size — number of instructions stored in the program ROM; if your instructions are 16-bit, you can also express this in bytes.

Hutchens directly demonstrates the usefulness of clock ticks, executed instructions, CPI, IPC, and branches in Logisim-based processor evaluation

CPI and IPC are reciprocal measures calculated from the same counts. They will not be treated as independent evidence of performance.
If the implemented processor completes one instruction per cycle, both may remain equal to one across workloads.
A lower CPI alone does not establish that one architecture completes a task more efficiently: the total instruction count must also be considered.
Cache statistics, branch-prediction misses, and pipeline-specific statistics are outside the evaluation scope.

Benchmark programs for testing (different types of computation).
Each algorithm will use the same input values and expected output across systems.

Measurment procedure.
Clock cycles will be counted using a defined CPU clock edge, rather than assuming that every simulator tick represents a full cycle.
Instructions will be counted when they complete.

Data-memory accesses will exclude instruction fetches. Reads will be counted when an instruction consumes a memory value, rather than whenever a combinational memory output changes.
Writes will be counted when a write is committed.

Comparing the systems and interpreting the results.
Comparison systems will be selected according to available source files, documentation, simulator accessibility, and support for the chosen workloads.
The analysis will examine register availability, instruction-set capabilities, datapath organisation, control logic, and memory interaction.
Observed differences will be supported by instruction listings and execution traces. Since multiple architectural features may differ simultaneously,
explanations will distinguish demonstrated behaviour from possible causes.
Cycle counts will not be presented as physical execution times. Simulator speed and configured tick frequency will not be used to claim hardware speed advantages.